title: "Creative Problem Solving: a divergence engine for open-ended problems"
message: "If you reference this work, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Golan
    given-names: Yaniv
    email: yaniv@golan.name
repository-code: "https://github.com/yaniv-golan/creative-problem-solving"
url: "https://github.com/yaniv-golan/creative-problem-solving"
abstract: >-
  An agent skill that generates non-obvious options for open-ended problems by attacking
  them from several unlike angles at once, each in an isolated sub-agent so the passes
  cannot drift toward one another, rather than a single brainstorm. Every run is grounded
  in what already exists, the prominently-presented options have their borrowed mechanisms
  checked by search, and stage integrity is enforced by a script rather than narrated.
  The design starts from published findings that branded creativity frameworks do not
  outperform a plain prompt with language models, and that denial of the previous move and
  distribution sampling do. Those are results from the literature, not from this
  repository: its own ablations could not separate the contribution of the mechanisms it
  removed from the grading noise floor, and the rebuilt fan-out pipeline has one blind
  comparison behind it on a single problem. What the repository does offer is the record —
  eval definitions, graded results, full run transcripts, pre-registered experiment
  designs, the inconclusive ablations, and the rounds where the skill lost to a plain
  prompt.
